# Week 5 Day 3 — Route 53 Hosted Zone & Routing Policies

## DNS Architecture

**Hosted zone:** `fintrust-lab.internal` — Public hosted zone (a real internal
service-discovery zone would be Private; Public was used here for lab
purposes since Route 53's health checkers need public reachability to
demonstrate failover behaviour).

| Record | Type | Target | Notes |
|--------|------|--------|-------|
| `app.fintrust-lab.internal` | Alias A | `fintrust-alb` | Free, auto-updates if the ALB's IP changes, works at any name level including the apex |
| `api.fintrust-lab.internal` | CNAME | ALB DNS name | Fixed TTL (300s), subdomain only — CNAME cannot point the zone apex itself |
| `canary.fintrust-lab.internal` (production) | Alias A, Weighted | `fintrust-alb` | Weight 90 |
| `canary.fintrust-lab.internal` (canary) | Alias A, Weighted | second ALB / same ALB for demo | Weight 10 |

## Why Alias, Not CNAME, for `app`

Alias records are AWS-specific and can point at AWS resources — ALBs,
CloudFront distributions, other Route 53 records — with two properties CNAME
doesn't have: they're **free** to query, and Route 53 **auto-updates** the
underlying IP if the ALB's address changes, so there's nothing to maintain
manually. CNAME would have worked here too since `app` is a subdomain, not
the zone apex — but Alias is strictly better for any AWS resource target, so
there's no real reason to prefer CNAME unless the target is a non-AWS
hostname.

## Why CNAME Was Still Used for `api`

Purely to demonstrate the record type — pointing `api` at the raw ALB DNS
name via CNAME works because `api` is a subdomain, never the root domain
`fintrust-lab.internal` itself. If this were pointing the **root domain** at
the ALB, CNAME would be invalid outright (RFC 1912 forbids a CNAME at the
zone apex) and Alias would be the only option.

## Routing Policy Decision Quiz

| # | Requirement | Correct Policy |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Route Nigeria → Lagos region, South Africa → Cape Town | **Geolocation** — routes by country/continent, not by measured network performance |
| 2 | 80% traffic to current production, 20% to a new version under test | **Weighted** — proportional split by assigned weight |
| 3 | Route to the server with the lowest ping time from the user's location | **Latency** — measures actual round-trip time, not political geography |
| 4 | Automatic failover if primary ALB health check fails | **Failover** — active/passive, requires a health check on the primary |
| 5 | Spread traffic equally across 3 ALBs at the DNS layer | **Weighted (equal weights)** or **Multivalue** — either distributes across multiple targets; Multivalue also strips out unhealthy targets automatically |
| 6 | Route all traffic to one IP, no health checks, no logic needed | **Simple** — the only policy that genuinely cannot support health checks |

## Group Discussion

**What is TTL propagation delay, and why does it matter for Failover routing?**
TTL is how long a resolver caches a DNS answer before it's allowed to ask
again. During that window, clients keep using the cached (possibly now-dead)
IP even after Route 53 has switched to the secondary record — the failover
decision happened, but the client hasn't found out yet. For Failover
records I'd set TTL to **60 seconds**, low enough that a real outage
resolves within roughly 2-3 minutes total (health check failure detection at
~90s + TTL expiry at 60s), without setting it so low that it generates
excessive DNS query volume during normal operation.

**FinTrust wants to block users from specific countries. Which policy, and can it fully block traffic?**
**Geolocation** is the policy, but it cannot *block* traffic outright — DNS
routing only controls which record a query resolves to. Geolocation can be
used to *not* return a normal record for a blocked country (e.g. resolve
them to a "service unavailable" page or a non-functional target), but a
sufficiently technical user could still reach the actual IP directly if they
already know it, or via a different DNS path. True blocking needs an
enforcement layer that can actually reject traffic — a WAF geo-match rule or
a Network ACL/Security Group with IP-based rules — DNS-layer geolocation is
a routing hint, not an access control.

**How does Latency routing differ from Geolocation, and can a South African user land on US East?**
Geolocation routes by *political* location — a country or continent mapped
to a specific record, regardless of actual network performance. Latency
routing ignores geography entirely and routes purely on measured round-trip
time to each Region. Yes — if for some reason the RTT from a South African
user's ISP happened to be lower to `us-east-1` than to `af-south-1` (unusual,
but possible depending on network peering and routing paths), Latency
routing would send them to US East. Geolocation would never do this, since
it doesn't measure performance at all.

## Individual Reflection

**Weighted routing configuration built, and how I'd adjust it for a gradual rollout:**
I set up `canary.fintrust-lab.internal` with two records sharing the same
name — `production` at weight 90 and `canary` at weight 10, both Alias
records pointing at ALB targets. Route 53 distributes roughly 10% of queries
to the canary record and 90% to production, proportional to the weights
(not an exact split on every single request, but converging to that ratio
over enough queries). For a gradual rollout from 0% → 10% → 50% → 100%, I'd
change only the weight values over time — e.g. start canary at weight 0
(production 100, canary 0 — nothing routes there yet), then canary 10 /
production 90, then canary 50 / production 50, and finally flip fully by
setting canary to 100 and production to 0 (or just deleting the production
record). No DNS record *names* change at any step — only the weight
numbers, which is what makes this pattern safe to adjust incrementally
without touching application configuration.

**When is Geolocation Proximity better than Geolocation?**
Plain Geolocation is rigid — a country or continent maps to exactly one
record, full stop. Geoproximity is useful when I need to *shift capacity*
between regions that are both geographically valid options — for example, if
`af-south-1` is nearing capacity and I want to push some West African
traffic toward a European region instead, without reclassifying any
country's political mapping. The "bias" value lets me expand or shrink a
region's effective coverage area gradually, which plain Geolocation has no
mechanism for at all — it's an all-or-nothing per-location assignment.

**"Can Route 53 replace my load balancer?"**
No — they operate at different layers and solve different problems. Route
53 makes a decision at the **DNS level**, before any connection is even
established — it just tells the client which IP to connect to. It has no
visibility into individual requests, can't inspect HTTP headers or paths,
and can't do session persistence. An ALB receives the actual TCP connection
and makes per-request decisions (path-based routing, TLS termination,
target health). In practice they work *together*, not as alternatives —
Route 53 points a domain name at an ALB's DNS name; it doesn't replace what
the ALB does once traffic actually arrives.
